{"name":"Dress watch","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Lanco_dress_watch%2C_anni_%2740.jpg/960px-Lanco_dress_watch%2C_anni_%2740.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lanco_dress_watch,_anni_%2740.jpg","caption":"Lanco dress watch, anni '40","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["A dress watch is a thin, simple wristwatch meant for business and formal wear. It adds a refined accessory to an outfit and signals polish at the wrist.","Wristwatches became standard menswear in the early twentieth century, and dress watches descend from that tradition. Style references describe them as thin, plain and often gold or leather-strapped."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People wear one with a suit, shirt cuffs or smart-casual outfit, usually on a leather strap, picking a slim case that slides under a cuff and a clean dial. Case size and strap colour are matched to shoes and belt."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["There are few health risks; costs range widely, and a thin case offers less water and shock resistance than a sports watch."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Lanco_dress_watch%2C_anni_%2740.jpg/960px-Lanco_dress_watch%2C_anni_%2740.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6d/Lanco_dress_watch%2C_anni_%2740.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Lanco dress watch, anni '40","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","creator":"Clyde94","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lanco_dress_watch,_anni_%2740.jpg","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ad/Vetta_dress_watch%2C_met%C3%A0_anni_Ottanta.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.or